  <section class="content-section" id="verdict">
    <p class="page-kicker">Quick verdict</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Boring, correct, still the default in 2026.</h2>
    <p>The Logitech C920x is the answer for 90% of "my webcam is bad" threads. It's a 1080p60 sensor with two decent microphones, works in every conferencing app without drivers, and looks noticeably better than every laptop built-in webcam. It is not a cinema camera, does not do 4K, does not have HDR — but it costs less than $80 and delivers a call quality that puts you in the top 20% of any Zoom / Teams / Slack meeting you're in.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">The problem it solves</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Built-in laptop webcams are dire.</h2>
    <p>Laptop webcams are the last thing manufacturers optimize. The sensor is tiny (usually 1MP or lower), the lens is plastic, the low-light performance is terrible. On a call, this shows up as grain, washed-out color, and unnatural skin tone. The C920x has a 1/4-inch sensor, a real glass lens, and Logitech's HDR-lite image processing that pulls detail out of shadows without blowing highlights.</p><p>The result: on a call from a normally-lit room, you look like you're on video. On the laptop cam, you look like you're on Skype in 2011.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="specs">
    <p class="page-kicker">Verified specs</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">What Logitech lists.</h2>
    <ul><li>1080p at 30fps standard, 1080p60 with H.264 SVC in supported apps</li><li>Autofocus with fixed-focus mode toggle in Logi Options+</li><li>78° field of view</li><li>Two omnidirectional microphones with stereo capture</li><li>USB-A connection (2.0 sufficient, 3.0 supported)</li><li>Universal clip mount for monitors up to ~2 inches thick, tripod thread on the base</li><li>Works with all conferencing apps that support UVC (Zoom, Teams, Slack, Meet, OBS, XSplit)</li><li>No driver install required for basic use</li></ul>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="compatibility">
    <p class="page-kicker">Compatibility check</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">It works with almost everything.</h2>
    <p>Windows, macOS, Linux, and ChromeOS all support USB Video Class (UVC) natively, and the C920x is UVC-compliant. No drivers needed for camera + microphone use. Install Logi Options+ (Windows/Mac) if you want image controls, framing, and firmware updates — everything else works out of the box.</p><p>Two limitations:</p><ul><li>1080p60 in H.264 SVC mode only works in apps that support it (Zoom, Teams, some OBS setups). Everywhere else you get 1080p30.</li><li>Some laptops with limited USB-A ports may need a hub. The C920x is USB-A only; there is no USB-C variant.</li></ul>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">Real-world use cases</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">The setup this makes look better.</h2>
    <p><strong>Daily video calls from home office.</strong> Puts you above the baseline of what everyone else on the call looks like. Especially noticeable if the room has one directional light source.</p><p><strong>Podcast video / talking-head recordings.</strong> 1080p60 is enough for YouTube. Skin tone and dynamic range are miles better than a phone selfie cam propped on a stand.</p><p><strong>Beginner streaming or teaching / tutoring.</strong> More than adequate for Twitch, YouTube Live, or Zoom classes. Upgrade to a mirrorless with a capture card only when your production actually needs it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">Tradeoffs and deal-breakers</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Where the C920x falls short.</h2>
    <ul><li><strong>Low-light performance is average.</strong> The sensor is 2013-era. A dark room still looks like a dark room. A cheap $20 desk light aimed at your face fixes this more than any webcam upgrade.</li><li><strong>Autofocus can hunt.</strong> Fixed-focus mode via Logi Options+ solves this if it bothers you.</li><li><strong>No 4K.</strong> The C922 Pro Stream also doesn't do 4K; step up to the Brio 4K for that. But 4K webcams are almost always upscaled 1080p sensors, so the value is questionable.</li><li><strong>USB-A only.</strong> If your laptop has only USB-C, you need a hub or an adapter.</li><li><strong>Microphone is fine, not great.</strong> Fine for calls where nobody has a real mic. Get a dedicated USB mic if audio quality actually matters.</li></ul>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="alternatives">
    <p class="page-kicker">Better alternatives</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">When to buy something else.</h2>
    <p><strong>iPhone Continuity Camera / DroidCam.</strong> If you have a modern iPhone or Android phone, the phone sensor is bigger than the C920x and produces better image quality when the phone is stable in a stand. Free (Continuity is built into macOS Ventura+; DroidCam has a Windows version). Downside: phone is unavailable during the call.</p><p><strong>Insta360 Link / Elgato Facecam Pro.</strong> $200-300. Real step up in low-light, better sensor, AI framing. Overkill for anyone not doing paid content work.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading"></h2>
    <div class="cards"><div class="card"><h3>Does it work with my Mac?</h3><p>Yes. macOS treats it as a standard UVC camera. No driver install.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Is the C920x different from the C920 Pro HD?</h3><p>Marginally. The C920x is the current retail packaging with a 3-month XSplit license bundled. Image quality and hardware are effectively the same.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Can I mount it on a tripod?</h3><p>Yes. Standard 1/4-20 tripod thread on the base.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Does it have a physical privacy shutter?</h3><p>No. Comes with a small clip-on cover included, or use a $3 aftermarket one.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>1080p60 or 4K30 — which matters more?</h3><p>Neither, in practice. 1080p30 is what actually gets used on 99% of video calls. 60fps looks smoother in recorded talking-head video.</p></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="buy-or-skip">
    <p class="page-kicker">Buy or skip</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading"></h2>
    <div class="cards"><div class="card"><h3>Buy this if</h3><p>Your laptop webcam is embarrassing on daily calls and you want a boring reliable fix. It's been the default answer for a decade for good reason.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Skip this if</h3><p>You do serious content work — a mirrorless camera plus capture card is the tier above and worth the difference. Or if you have a modern iPhone you're happy to tie up during calls.</p></div></div>
  </section>

